qa("E. Extensions (Phase 7)", "How do you compute PCA from the SVD and what do the explained variances mean?",
   f"Standardise and centre the features to get Z, then take the SVD Z = U S V'. The columns of V are the principal directions (loadings), the scores are Z V, and the variance along component j is s_j^2/(n-1); dividing by the sum gives the explained-variance ratio. For my set D (pooled, {npw}) the first component explains {val(E1S, dict(sample='pooled', fset='D', component=1.0), 'explained_var_ratio', '{:.2f}', label='PC1 explained D pooled')} of the variance and the first three together {val(E1S, dict(sample='pooled', fset='D', component=3.0), 'cum_explained', '{:.2f}', label='cumulative explained D pooled PC3')}; the numerical rank is {val(E1S, dict(sample='pooled', fset='D', component=1.0), 'numerical_rank', '{:.0f}', label='rank D pooled')} of {val(E1S, dict(sample='pooled', fset='D', component=1.0), 'n_features', '{:.0f}', label='n features D')}, so no feature is exactly redundant.")
qa(None, "Are estradiol and progesterone collinear? What did PCA show?",
   f"Only moderately: the Pearson correlation of log estradiol and log progesterone is {hk('pooled', 'log1p', 'all rows')} (pooled, n = {nh('pooled', 'all rows')} rows), so the first principal component of the two hormones holds {val(E1K, dict(sample='pooled', scale='log1p', group='all rows'), 'pc1_share_of_two_hormones', '{:.2f}', label='PC1 share two hormones pooled log1p')} of their variance (for two variables this share is (1+|r|)/2). Both hormones depend strongly on cycle phase, but they are not near-duplicates of each other, so PCA cannot compress them much.")
qa(None, "Why is ridge on all principal components identical to ridge on the original features?",
   f"V is an orthogonal matrix, i.e. a rotation. Rotating the features rotates the weight vector but leaves its length unchanged, and the ridge penalty only depends on the length, so the best predictions are the same. I verified it: over {agg(E1I, '', None, 'count', '{:.0f}', label='identity-check configurations')} configurations the largest difference between out-of-fold predictions was {agg(E1I, '', 'max_abs_pred_diff', 'max', '{:.1e}', label='max |prediction difference| ridge on PCs vs raw')}. Keeping only some components (PC regression) or fitting trees is different, because these are not rotation-invariant.")
qa(None, "Why must PCA be fitted inside the training fold, and did components help prediction?",
   f"PCA uses only the predictors, but the coordinate system still depends on every row used to compute it; if held-out women's rows were included, they would shape the components, a mild form of leakage. So the mean, standard deviation and loadings are computed from the training rows at every level (outer and inner folds) and held-out rows are only projected. Result: PC regression changed RMSE by a median of {calc('E1 pcr median delta RMSE', float(dd[dd['first'].str.startswith('pcr')].delta.median()), '{:+.4f}', E1D, 'median delta, first starts with pcr')} compared with raw ridge and was significantly better in {sel('pcr', ' and delta < 0 and hi < 0')} of {n_pcr} comparisons; PCA did not help prediction on this data.")
